2. Mathematical Preliminaries and State Space

2.1 Cognitive State as a Hybrid Object

Let \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}} be a fixed, finite‑complexity class of directed acyclic graphs with typed nodes (e.g., convolution, attention, memory read/write) and edges carrying tensor shapes. The boundedness condition is defined by an upper bound on graph depth, width, and operator repertoire; this restriction is essential for tractable verification (Section 2.5). For any \mathcal{G}\in\mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}}, let \Theta(\mathcal{G}) be a finite-dimensional Riemannian manifold of continuous parameters. The hybrid state space is

\mathcal{S} = \bigcup_{\mathcal{G}\in\mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}}} \big( \{\mathcal{G}\} \times \Theta(\mathcal{G}) \times \mathcal{H} \big),

where \mathcal{H} is a Polish space containing memory structures, world-model states, and meta‑cognitive variables. We equip \mathcal{S} with the Gromov–Wasserstein distance between behaviour distributions. For a fixed reference environment E_0, each state s induces a probability distribution \mu_s over output trajectories. Define

d(s,s') = \inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma(\mu_s,\mu_{s'})} \iint |\, \|F_s(x)-F_s(y)\| - \|F_{s'}(x')-F_{s'}(y')\| \,| \, d\gamma(x,x') \, d\gamma(y,y'),

where F_s maps trajectories to a feature space (e.g., final-layer activations) and \Gamma is the set of couplings. This metric makes \mathcal{S} a Polish space; continuity in d implies that small changes in architecture or parameters that do not alter behaviour are measured as close.

2.2 Stochastic Dynamics with Constraint Sets

The system evolves according to a projected stochastic approximation. At step t, let e_t \sim \mu_t be an environmental experience, and let \xi_t be internal randomness. The modification direction \Delta_t is generated by a reflection module and filtered by a verification oracle. The feasible set \mathcal{C}_t is defined below. With a diminishing step-size sequence \rho_t \to 0 satisfying \sum \rho_t = \infty, \sum \rho_t^2 < \infty, the update is

s_{t+1} = \Pi_{\mathcal{C}_t}\!\big( s_t + \rho_t \, \Delta_t(s_t, e_t, \xi_t) \big).

The projection \Pi_{\mathcal{C}_t} is taken with respect to the metric d. The initial state s_0 is fixed.

2.3 Utility and Performance

Let \pi be a distribution over tasks \tau from a measurable space \mathcal{T}. For each task, the expected loss of state s is U_\tau(s) = \mathbb{E}_{e\sim\mu_\tau}[\ell(s,e,\tau)], where \ell is a bounded Borel-measurable loss function. The aggregate utility is

U(s) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}\big[U_\tau(s)\big].

We assume U is bounded below and continuous with respect to d.

