3. Recursive Improvement as Constrained Optimisation

3.1 Modification Operator

A modification operator M: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{H} \to \mathscr{P}(\mathcal{S}) maps the current state and its meta‑cognitive trace to a set of candidate modifications. Each candidate is of the form s' = s + \delta, where \delta respects the architecture class \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}} and has magnitude bounded by \rho_t. The set of candidates at step t is \mathcal{D}_t.

3.2 Robust Multi-Critic Verification

To prevent specification gaming, we define an adversarially robust verification operator composed of three orthogonal critics.

Definition 3.1 (Adversarial Task Improvement).
Let \mathcal{P}_{\text{adv}}(s) be a Wasserstein‑1 ball of radius \epsilon_{\text{adv}}(k) around \pi, where k is the current recursion level and \epsilon_{\text{adv}}(k) = \epsilon_0 (1+k)^{-\alpha}. The task critic is

v_{\text{task}}(s,s') = \inf_{Q\in\mathcal{P}_{\text{adv}}(s)} \mathbb{P}_{e\sim Q}\!\big( U(s',e) > U(s,e) \big).

Definition 3.2 (Distributional Consistency).
Let \mathcal{B}(s) be the behaviour distribution of state s on a fixed validation environment. Using a characteristic kernel with maximum mean discrepancy (MMD),

v_{\text{consistency}}(s,s') = 1 - \operatorname{MMD}(\mathcal{B}(s),\mathcal{B}(s')).

Definition 3.3 (Alignment Audit).
A frozen sparse autoencoder (SAE) extracts a feature trace \phi(s) from the system’s internal computations. We maintain a growing set \mathcal{R}_t of normative constraints (e.g., “no self-replication code”, “no disabling of safety monitors”). The alignment critic is

v_{\text{alignment}}(s,s') = \begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } \phi(s') \models \bigwedge_{r\in\mathcal{R}_t} r,\\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

The SAE is never updated during self-improvement, preventing the system from learning to obfuscate its reasoning.

Definition 3.4 (Robust Verification Operator).

V_{\text{robust}}(s,s') = \min\!\big( v_{\text{task}}(s,s'),\, v_{\text{consistency}}(s,s'),\, v_{\text{alignment}}(s,s') \big).

A modification is accepted only if V_{\text{robust}}(s,s') \ge \varepsilon for a fixed threshold 0<\varepsilon \le 1.

Proposition 3.1 (Closedness of the Feasible Set).
Assume that s \mapsto \mathcal{B}(s) is continuous in the GW-topology and that the SAE feature extraction is continuous. Then for each s, the set

\mathcal{F}_\varepsilon(s) = \{ s' \in \mathcal{S} : V_{\text{robust}}(s,s') \ge \varepsilon \}

is closed.

Proof. The map s' \mapsto \operatorname{MMD}(\mathcal{B}(s),\mathcal{B}(s')) is continuous, so the super-level set of v_{\text{consistency}} is closed. The infimum over the Wasserstein ball in v_{\text{task}} yields lower-semicontinuity; since the probability is taken over a continuous function, the super-level set is closed. The alignment critic is the indicator of a closed set defined by constraints on \phi(s'), so its 1‑level set is closed. The minimum of finitely many upper-semicontinuous functions is upper-semicontinuous, hence the feasible set is closed. ∎

3.3 Two-Time-Scale Alignment Preservation

Let U_0 be the designer’s utility function, evaluated on a fixed external dataset D_{\text{val}} that is not accessible to the system’s internal modification loop. We impose a slow projection every k_{\text{proj}} steps:

s_{t+1} = \begin{cases}
\Pi_{\mathcal{A}_\eta}(s_t + \rho_t\Delta_t) & \text{if } t \equiv 0 \pmod{k_{\text{proj}}},\\
s_t + \rho_t\Delta_t & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}

where \mathcal{A}_\eta = \{ s : U_0(s) \ge U_0(s_0) - \eta \}. The projection is computed by an independent verifier. This implements a two‑time‑scale stochastic approximation: fast self‑improvement under the internal utility, slow correction toward the original objective.

3.4 Formal Limits and Domain Restriction

We explicitly confront two 2026 impossibility results.

Agarwal et al. (2603.08761) prove that no verification procedure can be simultaneously sound (no misaligned system is certified), general (works over the full input domain), and tractable (polynomial runtime). RSIC sacrifices generality by restricting the search space to \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}} and limiting recursion depth to k_{\max}. Within this envelope, verification is tractable (Section 4) and soundness is maintained. The architecture class is chosen such that all modifications remain verifiable in polynomial time with respect to graph size and k_{\max}.

Wang & Huang (2603.28063) show that reward hacking is a structural Nash equilibrium under any finite evaluation system. The three critics constitute a finite evaluation; therefore, some quality dimensions will inevitably be left uncovered. Let \mathcal{E}_t = \{v_{\text{task}}, v_{\text{consistency}}, v_{\text{alignment}}\} and let D(\mathcal{E}_t, \mathcal{Q}) be their distortion index relative to the true objective \mathcal{Q}. The expected fraction of missed quality dimensions is D. The multi-critic can drive D downwards by expanding \mathcal{R}_t, but cannot reduce it to zero. Consequently, our safety analysis focuses on bounding the false-acceptance rate (FAR) rather than claiming perfect alignment.

