4. Complexity Analysis of Verification with Caching and Noise

4.1 Branching Process Model

We model the verification cost across recursion levels as a branching process with memory. At recursion level i, a modification triggers re-evaluation of lower-level behaviours. With a cache storing fingerprints of previously verified state pairs, only novel modifications require full verification. Let \nu_i be the novelty rate at level i—the probability that a candidate is not resolved from cache. For analytical tractability, we assume \nu_i = \nu constant per level. The number of full verifications at level i, denoted C_i, satisfies

C_i = 2\nu \, C_{i-1} + \sigma_v \sqrt{C_{i-1}} \, \zeta_i,

where \zeta_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) represents stochastic verification noise (e.g., finite-sample MMD estimation errors), and \sigma_v is its standard deviation. The factor 2 arises because each modification potentially affects interactions across two lower-level components.

4.2 Expected Cost and Maximum Recursion Depth

Taking expectations,

\mathbb{E}[C_i] = 2\nu \, \mathbb{E}[C_{i-1}].

Thus, if \nu > 1/2, the expected cost grows exponentially with base 2\nu; if \nu < 1/2, it decays exponentially. For a total verification budget C_{\text{budget}},

k_{\max} \le \frac{\log(C_{\text{budget}} / \gamma)}{\log(2\nu)},

where \gamma is the base cost of a single verification.

Theorem 4.1 (Effective Recursion Depth with Noise).
Assume \nu > 1/2 and that the noise term is independent across levels with variance \sigma_v^2. Then, with high probability, the actual number of levels that can be completed before budget exhaustion satisfies

k_{\max} = \frac{\log(C_{\text{budget}} / \gamma)}{\log(2\nu)} - O\!\left( \frac{\sigma_v}{\log(2\nu)} \right).

Proof sketch. The process is a supercritical branching process with immigration (the noise term acts as an additive perturbation). Standard large-deviation estimates for branching processes yield that the total cost concentrates around its expectation, with fluctuations proportional to the noise magnitude. The correction term is derived via Chernoff bounds on the sum of log-costs. ∎

4.3 Explaining the Empirical Plateau

Current systems operate with \nu close to 1 (most modifications are novel) and non-negligible \sigma_v. For \nu = 0.85 and \sigma_v = 0.2, numerical evaluation of the bounds gives k_{\max} \in [2,4] for realistic budgets, matching the observed plateau. As caching improves (\nu decreases) and noise reduces, deeper recursion becomes feasible. Our empirical roadmap measures \nu and \sigma_v directly.

