6. Convergence Analysis

6.1 Assumptions

Assumption 6.1 (Martingale Difference Noise).
The modification direction \Delta_t can be written as \Delta_t = \nabla_{\mathcal{F}} U(s_t) + \eta_t + \xi_{v,t}, where:

· \nabla_{\mathcal{F}} U(s_t) is the projected gradient of U onto the feasible set \mathcal{F}_\varepsilon,
· \eta_t is a martingale difference sequence with \mathbb{E}[\eta_t \mid \mathcal{F}_t] = 0 and bounded variance,
· \xi_{v,t} is the verification noise, satisfying \mathbb{E}[\xi_{v,t} \mid \mathcal{F}_t] = 0 and \operatorname{Var}(\xi_{v,t}) \le \sigma_v^2.

Assumption 6.2 (Feasible Set Regularity).
The feasible set \mathcal{F}_\varepsilon is closed, convex in expectation, and contains at least one stationary point of U. Its interior is non-empty.

Assumption 6.3 (Step Sizes).
\rho_t = c / t^\gamma with 0 < \gamma \le 1, so that \sum \rho_t = \infty and \sum \rho_t^2 < \infty.

6.2 Main Result

Theorem 6.1 (Almost-Sure Convergence to a Neighbourhood).
Under Assumptions 6.1–6.3, the sequence \{s_t\} generated by RSIC‑0 converges almost surely to a set \mathcal{S}^* where

\| \nabla_{\mathcal{F}} U(s) \| \le C(\sigma_v + \eta_{\text{proj}} + \zeta_{\text{arch}}),

with C>0 a constant, \eta_{\text{proj}} the alignment tolerance, and \zeta_{\text{arch}} the expressiveness gap of \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}}. In particular,

\liminf_{t\to\infty} U(s_t) \ge U(s^*) - O(\sigma_v + \eta + \zeta_{\text{arch}})

almost surely, where s^* is the globally optimal configuration within \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}}.

Proof. Consider the continuous-time interpolation of \{s_t\}. The two‑time‑scale structure (fast modification, slow alignment projection) decouples: on the fast time scale, the alignment projection acts as a stationary constraint. The ODE associated with the fast dynamics is

\dot{s} = \nabla_{\mathcal{F}} U(s) + \xi_v(s),

where \xi_v(s) is a bounded stationary noise process arising from verification errors. Standard stochastic approximation theory (Kushner & Yin, 2003; Borkar, 2024) guarantees that the limit set of the interpolated process is contained in the internally chain transitive set of this ODE. Because \mathcal{F}_\varepsilon is closed and contains a stationary point, and the noise term averages to zero in the ODE limit, the trajectory is absorbed into a neighbourhood of the stationary set whose radius is governed by the noise amplitude \sigma_v. The slow projection every k_{\text{proj}} steps acts as a contraction toward \mathcal{A}_\eta; by the two‑time‑scale analysis of Chandak et al. (2025), the asymptotic coupling of the fast process remains within O(\eta) of the original objective. The gap \zeta_{\text{arch}} is the distance between the true optimum in the unrestricted space and the best element of \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}}, which cannot be eliminated. The claimed bound follows. ∎

Remark 6.1 (Role of Verification Noise).
If verification were noiseless (\sigma_v = 0), the limit would be an exact stationary point of U within the feasible set. Noise introduces a residual error; reducing \sigma_v (e.g., by larger MMD samples or more accurate world models) directly improves the asymptotic guarantee.

