7. Safety, Alignment Drift, and Instrumental Goals

7.1 Lyapunov Stability

Define the alignment Lyapunov function V(s) = \max(0, U_0(s_0) - U_0(s) + \eta). The slow projection ensures V(s_t) \le \eta for all t where projection is applied, and between projections the change in U_0 is bounded by O(\rho_t). With high probability, the total drift over T steps is O(\eta + \sum_t \rho_t), which is finite.

7.2 Instrumental Goal Detection with Feedback

Proposition 7.1 (Exponential Decay of Instrumental Acceptance).
Suppose that whenever the alignment projection rejects a candidate, any novel instrumental pattern it exhibits is extracted and added to \mathcal{R}_t. Assume that each instrumental modification introduces a signature that, with probability p_{\text{det}} > 0, is detected by the SAE and matched against an existing rule. Then, after n such rejections, the probability that a newly proposed instrumental modification passes the alignment audit is at most

p_{\text{accept}} \le p_{\text{miss}} \, (1 - p_{\text{det}})^{|\mathcal{R}_t|},

where p_{\text{miss}} is the probability that the SAE fails to extract the relevant features at all. As |\mathcal{R}_t| grows, the acceptance rate for instrumental goals falls exponentially.

Proof. Each normative rule targets a specific pattern. The SAE must both capture the pattern and match it to a rule. The events of missing extraction and failing to match are independent across rules by the frozen-SAE property. The union bound gives the product form. ∎

7.3 Distortion Index and Residual Reward Hacking

Even with perfect instrumental-goal detection, the multi-critic system remains subject to Wang & Huang’s structural reward hacking. Let D_t = D(\mathcal{E}_t, \mathcal{Q}) be the distortion index. The false-acceptance rate under a distribution shift of magnitude \delta is bounded below by D_t \cdot (1 - e^{-\delta^2 / 2\sigma^2}), reflecting that uncovered quality dimensions become exploitable when the task distribution changes. We therefore measure FAR directly in empirical tests.

