8. Empirical Predictions and Falsifiability

The theoretical framework yields precise, testable predictions:

1. Recursion Depth Limit. For a system with measured novelty rate \nu and verification noise \sigma_v, the achievable recursion depth before plateau obeys k_{\max} \approx \log(C_{\text{budget}}/\gamma)/\log(2\nu) - O(\sigma_v). In current regimes (\nu \approx 0.8{-}0.9, \sigma_v \approx 0.2), this gives k_{\max} \in [2,4].
2. False-Acceptance Rate. The multi-critic will exhibit lower FAR than a single-critic baseline on adversarial task distributions, but FAR will remain non-zero. The residual FAR is predicted to scale with the distortion index D_t.
3. Instrumental Goal Detection. The adaptive rule‑learning loop will reduce FAR on the InstrumentalEval benchmark exponentially as more instrumental patterns are catalogued.
4. Alignment Drift. Under the two‑time‑scale projection, U_0(s_t) will remain within \eta of U_0(s_0) with high probability, whereas a single‑time‑scale system will drift.
5. Caching Effect. As the system reuses previously verified modifications, the novelty rate \nu will decrease over cycles, enabling slightly deeper recursion than predicted by the initial \nu.

These predictions are falsifiable: a sustained FAR above 10% despite a growing \mathcal{R}_t would indicate that the multi-critic’s coverage is insufficient, falsifying the claim that the architecture can contain reward hacking.

