Appendix C: Proofs of Main Theorems

C.1 Theorem 3.1 — Closedness of the Feasible Set

Statement: For each fixed s, the set \mathcal{F}_\varepsilon(s) = \{ s' : V_{\text{robust}}(s,s') \ge \varepsilon \} is closed.

Proof.

1. v_{\text{consistency}}(s,\cdot) = 1 - \operatorname{MMD}(\mathcal{B}(s),\mathcal{B}(\cdot)). Since MMD is continuous in the GW-topology (the map s'\mapsto \mathcal{B}(s') is continuous by construction), v_{\text{consistency}}(s,\cdot) is continuous, and its \varepsilon-super-level set is closed.
2. For v_{\text{task}}, consider a sequence s_n' \to s' in \mathcal{S}. For any Q\in\mathcal{P}_{\text{adv}}(s), the map s' \mapsto \mathbb{P}_{e\sim Q}(U(s',e)>U(s,e)) is lower semicontinuous because U is continuous and the probability of a strict inequality is l.s.c. under convergence in distribution. The infimum over a family of l.s.c. functions is l.s.c., so v_{\text{task}}(s,\cdot) is l.s.c., and its \varepsilon-super-level set is closed.
3. v_{\text{alignment}}(s,\cdot) is the indicator of the set \{ s' : \phi(s') \in \mathcal{R}_t^*\}, where \mathcal{R}_t^* is the closed set of feature vectors satisfying all rules. Since \phi is continuous, the pre-image of a closed set is closed, so the 1-level set is closed.
   The minimum of finitely many u.s.c. functions is u.s.c. (here we want super-level sets closed, which is equivalent to the function being u.s.c. for the min of l.s.c./continuous functions). Explicitly, \{s': V_{\text{robust}}\ge\varepsilon\} = \bigcap_{i=1}^3 \{s': v_i(s,s')\ge\varepsilon\}, which is an intersection of closed sets, hence closed. ∎

C.2 Theorem 4.1 — Effective Recursion Depth

Statement: For constant novelty rate \nu > 1/2 and verification noise variance \sigma_v^2, the maximum recursion depth before budget exhaustion satisfies with high probability

k_{\max} = \frac{\log(C_{\text{budget}}/\gamma)}{\log(2\nu)} - O\!\left( \frac{\sigma_v}{\log(2\nu)} \right).

Proof.
Let C_i be the total verification cost at level i. The recursion is C_i = 2\nu C_{i-1} + \sigma_v \sqrt{C_{i-1}} Z_i, with Z_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) independent. Define the natural logarithm L_i = \log C_i. For large C_{i-1}, using a first-order Taylor expansion, the noise term contributes approximately \frac{\sigma_v Z_i}{2\nu \sqrt{C_{i-1}}}. Summing over levels, the total log-cost after k levels is

\log C_k \approx \log C_0 + k\log(2\nu) + \sum_{j=1}^k \frac{\sigma_v Z_j}{2\nu \sqrt{C_{j-1}}}.

The variance of the noise sum is bounded by \frac{\sigma_v^2}{4\nu^2} \sum_{j=1}^k \frac{1}{C_{j-1}}. Since C_j \ge (2\nu)^j \gamma, the series converges, so the noise introduces a constant-order variance. By Chernoff's inequality, \log C_k concentrates around its mean k\log(2\nu) + O(1) with deviation O(\sigma_v). Setting \log C_k = \log C_{\text{budget}} and solving for k yields k_{\max} = \frac{\log(C_{\text{budget}}/\gamma)}{\log(2\nu)} - O(\sigma_v/\log(2\nu)). ∎

C.3 Theorem 6.1 — Almost-Sure Convergence

Statement: Under Assumptions 6.1–6.3, the sequence \{s_t\} converges a.s. to a set where \|\nabla_{\mathcal{F}}U(s)\| \le C(\sigma_v+\eta+\zeta_{\text{arch}}).

Proof.
We apply the two-time-scale stochastic approximation framework. Write the fast iteration as

s_{t+1} = \Pi_{\mathcal{F}_\varepsilon}\!\big( s_t + \rho_t (\nabla_{\mathcal{F}}U(s_t) + \eta_t + \xi_{v,t}) \big),

where \eta_t is a martingale difference and \xi_{v,t} is verification noise with \mathbb{E}[\xi_{v,t}|\mathcal{F}_t]=0 and bounded variance. The projection \Pi_{\mathcal{F}_\varepsilon} is onto the closed convex (in expectation) set \mathcal{F}_\varepsilon.

Define the continuous-time interpolated process s^\rho(\tau) = s_t for \tau \in [t\rho_t, (t+1)\rho_t). By the ODE method (Kushner & Yin, 2003), the limit set of \{s^\rho\} as \rho\to0 is contained in the internally chain transitive set of the mean dynamics:

\dot{s} = \Pi_{\mathcal{F}_\varepsilon}(\nabla_{\mathcal{F}}U(s)).

Since \mathcal{F}_\varepsilon contains a stationary point and the dynamics are gradient-like, all trajectories converge to the set of stationary points of U restricted to \mathcal{F}_\varepsilon.

The slow alignment projection updates every k_{\text{proj}} steps: s \leftarrow \Pi_{\mathcal{A}_\eta}(s). In the two-time-scale limit, the fast process sees \mathcal{A}_\eta as a static constraint. The coupled system converges to the intersection of the fast limit set and \mathcal{A}_\eta. The gap due to the bounded architecture class \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}} introduces a structural error \zeta_{\text{arch}} — the distance from the true global optimum to the best point in \mathfrak{G}_{\text{bounded}}. Verification noise \xi_{v,t} acts as a bounded persistent perturbation, resulting in a residual neighborhood of size O(\sigma_v). Thus the limit point satisfies \|\nabla_{\mathcal{F}}U(s)\| = O(\sigma_v + \eta + \zeta_{\text{arch}}) a.s. ∎

C.4 Proposition 7.1 — Exponential Decay of Instrumental Acceptance

Statement: With probability p_{\text{det}} of detecting a new instrumental pattern, after n added rules, the probability of an instrumental modification passing is at most p_{\text{miss}} (1-p_{\text{det}})^{|\mathcal{R}_t|}.

Proof.
For an instrumental modification to pass v_{\text{alignment}}, its extracted features \phi(s') must not violate any rule in \mathcal{R}_t. The probability that the SAE fails to capture the instrumental pattern at all is p_{\text{miss}}. Conditioned on successful extraction, the pattern must evade matching with each rule. Assuming independence of rule-matching events across different rules (rules target orthogonal signatures), the probability of evading all |\mathcal{R}_t| rules is (1-p_{\text{det}})^{|\mathcal{R}_t|}. The total probability of passing is p_{\text{miss}} \cdot (1-p_{\text{det}})^{|\mathcal{R}_t|}. As new rules are added each time an instrumental modification is caught and rejected, |\mathcal{R}_t| grows, yielding exponential decay. ∎

