Appendix E: Supplementary Lemmas

Lemma E.1 (Continuity of Behaviour Map).
If the architecture \mathcal{G} and parameters \theta vary continuously in the sense that the induced function f_s on inputs changes continuously in operator norm, then the map s \mapsto \mu_s is continuous in the GW-topology.

Proof. The Gromov–Wasserstein distance is controlled by the distortion of the internal metric and the change in the output distribution. For small perturbations in \mathcal{G},\theta, the internal metric d_s changes continuously (as activations shift slightly), and the output distribution \mu_s changes continuously in Wasserstein sense, so GW_2(\mu_s, \mu_{s'}) \to 0 as s' \to s. ∎

Lemma E.2 (Lipschitz Property of MMD under GW).
There exists a constant L such that for any s,s', |\operatorname{MMD}(\mathcal{B}(s),\cdot) - \operatorname{MMD}(\mathcal{B}(s'),\cdot)| \le L \cdot d(s,s').

Proof. MMD is Lipschitz with respect to the Wasserstein distance for characteristic kernels. Since GW dominates a certain Wasserstein distance between the push-forward distributions under the internal distance matrices, the result follows. ∎

Lemma E.3 (Super-level Set Closedness of infimum over Q).
Let f(s',Q) be lower semicontinuous in s' and continuous in Q. Then v(s') = \inf_{Q\in\mathcal{P}} f(s',Q) is lower semicontinuous.

Proof. For any \alpha, \{s': v(s') > \alpha\} = \bigcup_{Q\in\mathcal{P}} \{s': f(s',Q) > \alpha\}. Since each set in the union is open (by l.s.c. of f(\cdot,Q)), the union is open, so the super-level sets are open, establishing l.s.c. ∎

